IMPORT prelude

-- ccc14-helpers.l4 -- three-valued combinators for ccc14 (Criminal Code, Parts XII, XII.2 and XIII).
-- These belong to no provision, so they carry the prefix `ccc14 ` instead of a section label (a name no other part can define).
-- They are hoist candidates for the lead's merge (see NEEDS-CORE.md). They add no law: they only combine two answers
-- that other functions gave, keeping "the text says no" (JUST FALSE) apart from "not covered" (NOTHING).
-- Strong Kleene: a JUST FALSE decides an AND; a JUST TRUE decides an OR; otherwise a NOTHING stays NOTHING.

GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN, b IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`ccc14 both answers hold` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER b
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): both answers were taken from other provisions and are themselves not covered
        WHEN JUST y THEN
            IF y
            THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the answer a was not covered, so a yes from b does not decide it
            ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): carried unchanged from the answer b, which was itself a no
    WHEN JUST x THEN
        IF x
        THEN b
        ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): carried unchanged from the answer a, which was itself a no

GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN, b IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`ccc14 either answer holds` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER b
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): both answers were taken from other provisions and are themselves not covered
        WHEN JUST y THEN
            IF y
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the answer a was not covered, so a no from b does not decide it
    WHEN JUST x THEN
        IF x
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE b
